    <h2>How to choose for a newborn</h2>
    <p><strong>Start with suction control, not suction power.</strong> A newborn's nose is small and delicate, and the goal is to clear enough mucus to help them feed and sleep — not maximum suction. Manual control (NoseFrida) or a low, quiet electric setting (Grownsy SP-1) both get there; a 65 kPa top setting is not a selling point for a newborn.</p>
    <p><strong>Count the steps at 3AM, not in daylight.</strong> The aspirator that wins is the one you will actually use when you are exhausted. That is why cleaning reset time is our first metric: the NoseFrida's 60–90 seconds against the Grownsy's 120–180 seconds is a real difference at night, and the electric's warning card — switch off immediately, keep liquid out, keep the chamber dry — is one more thing to remember.</p>
    <p><strong>Plan for the filter or the chamber.</strong> Manual aspirators have an ongoing consumable: hygiene filters. Electric aspirators have an ongoing chore: drying the chamber and keeping liquid out of the machine. Neither is free; pick the cost you would rather pay. And do not use an aspirator on dried mucus alone — stubborn dried mucus still needs softening first, usually with saline (ask your pediatrician which product and how to use it).</p>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>What is the best nasal aspirator for a newborn?</h3>
    <p>For most parents, the manual Frida Baby NoseFrida: it is the fastest to clean in our testing (about 60–90 seconds), completely silent, and you control the suction so you can keep it gentle. If mouth suction is a deal-breaker, the electric GROWNSY is the tested alternative — easier to start, about 46 dB on its lowest setting, but slower to clean up.</p>
    <h3>Is an electric nasal aspirator safe for newborns?</h3>
    <p>It depends on the model and the setting. The models we tested use a low setting that is quiet and gentle, but not every electric aspirator is marketed for newborns — the Momcozy BreezyClear, for example, is listed for ages 2–12. Check the manufacturer's age range, use the lowest effective setting, and ask your pediatrician if you are unsure. This article is not medical advice.</p>
    <h3>How often can I use a nasal aspirator on my newborn?</h3>
    <p>We do not give medical guidance on frequency. In general, parents use an aspirator when congestion is visibly interfering with feeding or sleep, not on a fixed schedule, and overuse can irritate a newborn's nose. Ask your pediatrician for guidance for your baby.</p>
    <h3>Do I need to buy filters or spare tips?</h3>
    <p>For the NoseFrida, yes: disposable hygiene filters are part of every use and are an ongoing cost. For electric models, budget for spare silicone tips and the time to dry the chamber after each use. The Braun ships with two tip sizes, which is useful for a newborn.</p>

